<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.14.1: Distinguish Linear and Exponential Growth</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01498</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.14.1: Distinguish Linear and Exponential Growth</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9d0cab39-2dfc-440e-97bf-20a4906aa58a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">
      <title>Warm Up</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Here is a graph.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
        <media alt="Line graph with x-axis labeled 0 to 7 and y-axis labeled 0 to 200. The graph shows a steadily increasing line starting just below 125 on the y-axis and ending at about 150 at x = 7.">
          <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_16_02_01.svg"/>
        </media>

      </para>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Which equation do you think the graph represents? Use the graph to
            support your reasoning.</para>
          <list id="list-00003">
            <item><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>120</m:mn>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3.7</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
          <item><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>120</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1.03</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
          </list>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00007"> All answers are acceptable if backed up by a reasonable
            explanation. Compare your answer: <list id="list-00001">
              <item>The graph is for <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>120</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3.7</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> because it
            appears linear. On the graph, when <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>, the value of <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> appears to be
            a little less than 150. When <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>, <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>120</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3.7</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>145.9</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>, so the
            slope of the line could be 3.7.</item>
              <item>The graph is for <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>120</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1.03</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    </m:msup>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>.
            Multiplying 100 by 1.03 seven times, or <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>100</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1.03</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>,
            gives approximately 147.6, which more or less concurs with what is on the graph.</item>
              <item>It is hard to tell. I tried finding the <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>-coordinate
            values for different values of <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> using both
            equations, and they more or less agree with what is on the graph.</item>
            </list>
          </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">What information might help you decide more easily whether the graph
            represents a linear or an exponential function?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00010">Your answers may vary, but here are some examples:</para>
          <list id="list-00002">
            <item>Exact coordinates of points on the graph with whole-number <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>-values</item>
            <item>A larger graphing window showing larger domain values so that the behavior of the
              function can be more visible</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>
  </content>
</document>